{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-32548" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ -9 & 4\\end{aligned}]$ has the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{22}=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067467/state.json" "file_sha256" "2809266ce932829aa3b797a2314c60e3565c601b4b353c979da5698e463591ba"} {"question" "q-32548" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ -9 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=3,{u}_{22}=1,$ and ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{22}=3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067467/state.json" "file_sha256" "2809266ce932829aa3b797a2314c60e3565c601b4b353c979da5698e463591ba"} {"question" "q-32548" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067467/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c6d0ab4f9268972d2576ce36d1b76b115683962919e26c671fe790e709ba98d8"} {"question" "q-32548" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067467/state.json" "file_sha256" "2809266ce932829aa3b797a2314c60e3565c601b4b353c979da5698e463591ba"} {"question" "q-32548" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-3"] ["math" "-9"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "3"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067467/state.json" "file_sha256" "2809266ce932829aa3b797a2314c60e3565c601b4b353c979da5698e463591ba"} {"question" "q-32548" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067467/state.json" "file_sha256" "2809266ce932829aa3b797a2314c60e3565c601b4b353c979da5698e463591ba"} {"question" "q-32597" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -6\\end{aligned}]$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067467/state.json" "file_sha256" "2809266ce932829aa3b797a2314c60e3565c601b4b353c979da5698e463591ba"} {"question" "q-32597" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We need to reduce the matrix to the row echelon form. For every elementary row operation we also write the equivalent notation in terms of multiplication by the corresponding elementary matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=-2,{u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=-2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067467/state.json" "file_sha256" "2809266ce932829aa3b797a2314c60e3565c601b4b353c979da5698e463591ba"} {"question" "q-32597" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067467/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c6d0ab4f9268972d2576ce36d1b76b115683962919e26c671fe790e709ba98d8"} {"question" "q-32597" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067467/state.json" "file_sha256" "2809266ce932829aa3b797a2314c60e3565c601b4b353c979da5698e463591ba"} {"question" "q-32597" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2"] ["math" "-3"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067467/state.json" "file_sha256" "2809266ce932829aa3b797a2314c60e3565c601b4b353c979da5698e463591ba"